### Install the Base OS onto Compute Nodes

In stateful mode the compute nodes are provisioned before OpenHPC components
are added: associate the install image with the *compute* group, then boot the
nodes so they run the distribution installer over the network, perform an
unattended install onto local disk, and reboot into the freshly installed OS.

The steps that follow install packages directly on the running compute nodes
with the package manager, which requires the nodes to reach the distribution,
EPEL, and OpenHPC package repositories. By default xCAT points the compute
default route at the head node; ensure this provides a path to the
repositories (for example via NAT on the head node or a site gateway
registered in the xCAT `networks` table), or host a local repository mirror
on the head node and register it on the computes instead.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_if [ "${xcat_mode}" = "stateful" ] -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Associate the install image and boot the compute nodes -->
```bash
# Associate provisioning image for compute group
nodeset compute osimage=${xcat_osimage}
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Before power cycling, mark the OS that is currently running, if any. `/run` is
a tmpfs, so the marker cannot survive a reboot. The wait further below refuses
any node that still carries it, which stops a slow BMC reset from letting the
system that is about to be replaced satisfy the check when nodes are being
reinstalled rather than installed for the first time.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Mark the pre-reset OS so it cannot satisfy the wait below -->
<!-- ohpc_command xdsh compute "touch /run/ohpc-preprovision" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Set nodes to netboot and power cycle -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set has_ipmi -->
```bash
# Set compute group to boot from network on next boot
rsetboot compute net

# Power cycle compute nodes
rpower compute reset
```
<!-- ohpc_else -->
<!-- ohpc_command # No BMC/IPMI available -- signal external reset handler -->
<!-- ohpc_command for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)) ; do -->
<!-- ohpc_command   echo "#<<< ohpc_reset:$i,${c_name[$i]},${c_bmc[$i]} >>>#" -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

The stateful installation typically completes in 5 to 10 minutes, depending on
BIOS post times and disk speed. Monitor provisioning with the `rcons` command,
which displays the serial console for a selected node; the escape sequence to
exit `rcons` is `Ctrl-e c .` typed sequentially. Deployment progress can also
be queried with `nodestat compute`, which reports the installation state of
each node until an `sshd` response indicates the node is up.

Wait until every compute node has completed the installation and finished
booting before proceeding. `sshd` starts before the rest of the boot completes,
so waiting only for it lets the configuration steps below run against a node
that still refuses logins and has not finished starting systemd. The loop below
therefore requires every node to positively report a finished boot *and* to have
lost the pre-reset marker, so that neither an unreachable node nor one still
running the previous OS counts as ready. It gives up after `${install_timeout}`
seconds (two hours by default) rather than waiting forever on an installation
that failed:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Wait for compute nodes to complete the stateful install -->
<!-- ohpc_command # Bound the wait so a failed install cannot hang the recipe forever. -->
<!-- ohpc_command install_timeout=${install_timeout:-7200} -->
<!-- ohpc_command deadline=$(( SECONDS + install_timeout )) -->
<!-- ohpc_command while true; do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     booted=$(nodestat compute | grep -c ": sshd" || true) -->
<!-- ohpc_command     ready=0 -->
<!-- ohpc_command     if [ "$booted" -eq "$num_computes" ]; then -->
<!-- ohpc_command         # Require every node to report a finished boot. Counting -->
<!-- ohpc_command         # positives means a refused or lost connection leaves the -->
<!-- ohpc_command         # count short and keeps waiting, rather than reading as ready. -->
<!-- ohpc_command         ready=$(xdsh compute "test ! -e /run/ohpc-preprovision \ -->
<!-- ohpc_command             && systemctl is-system-running" 2>/dev/null \ -->
<!-- ohpc_command             | grep -cE ": (running|degraded)" || true) -->
<!-- ohpc_command         if [ "$ready" -eq "$num_computes" ]; then -->
<!-- ohpc_command             break -->
<!-- ohpc_command         fi -->
<!-- ohpc_command     fi -->
<!-- ohpc_command     if [ "$SECONDS" -ge "$deadline" ]; then -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "ERROR: after ${install_timeout}s, $booted of $num_computes nodes" >&2 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "reported sshd and $ready of $num_computes finished booting." >&2 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "Inspect with: nodestat compute; rcons <node>" >&2 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         exit 1 -->
<!-- ohpc_command     fi -->
<!-- ohpc_command     echo "$booted of $num_computes compute nodes ready" -->
<!-- ohpc_command     sleep 30 -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Once installed, verify access with a parallel command across the compute nodes
using `xdsh`, xCAT's native distributed shell:

```bash
xdsh compute uptime
c1:  12:56:50 up  4 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.01, 0.04
c2:  12:56:50 up  3 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.02, 0.05
```
